INT. CAROUSEL WORKSHOP - DAY

A barn of painted horses without riders. Dusty sunlight slants through high windows onto hooves, manes and half-stripped brass poles.

LOU PELLEGRINO, 60s, apron over a wool vest, sands the flank of a wooden palomino. A STRANGER, 40s, trim gray overcoat, fedora in hand, waits at the door and does not come in.

STRANGER
Mr. Pellegrino?

LOU
Depends who's asking.

STRANGER
A man who admires your horses.

LOU
Then he can buy one. Twelve dollars, the small ones.

The Stranger crosses to a bench and picks up a carved ear, turns it over.

STRANGER
I was told you carve other things too. Things that hold paper.

Lou keeps sanding. A long, even stroke.

LOU
Who told you?

STRANGER
Hollis. Walter Hollis. That's my name, since you'll want it.

LOU
(setting down the sandpaper)
Well, Mr. Hollis. Sit down. The horse isn't going anywhere.

HOLLIS lowers himself onto a stool beside the palomino.

HOLLIS
Nobody's hurrying.
